Dual-Channel, Molecular-Sieving Core/shell ZIF $@$ MOF Architectures as Engineered Fillers in Hybrid Membranes for Highly Selective $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ Separation
†The Molecular Foundry, Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, Berkeley, CA, 94720, United States ‡Civil and Environmental Engineering, University of California, Berkeley, CA, 94720, United States #Advanced Light Source, Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, Berkeley, CA, 94720, United States
KEYWORDS: Metal organic frameworks, core/shell, hybrid membrane, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture
## TEXT
Many types of aqueous alkanolamine solutions (amine scrubbers) have been studied and employed in conventional $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ removal via absorption processes. These liquid-based processes capture $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ with a high selectivity, but suffer in their regeneration step due to the high energy penalty to reverse the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ -amine binding reaction.10, 11 Moreover, the corrosive and volatile properties of amine solutions may result in additional pollutant emissions.12, 13 The regeneration energy required for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture by physical adsorption using porous materials can be significantly reduced compared to aqueous amine-based absorption processes due to the intrinsically lower heat capacities of solids than water.14 However, these solid sorbents are typically prepared as powders that can be difficult to implement $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture at a viable industrial scale due to the high pressure drops and poor heat transfer issues.15
Relative to above-mentioned conventional approaches, membrane-based gas separation processes have attracted intense interest because of their superior cost-effectiveness, environmental friendliness, and
## Nano Letters
simplicity for post-combustion $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture.16 Historically, polymers have been the dominant commercial materials used in gas separations due to their ease of fabrication and scalable production, and relatively low cost.17 Unfortunately, there is a traditional trade-off between permeability and selectivity for polymer membranes: highly selective membranes tend to have low permeability and vice versa. Inorganic membranes, on the other hand, have the potential to offer a better balance between permeability and selectivity.18 However, they suffer from problems of brittleness, high cost, and complex fabrication processes that still require further optimization.19 In order to overcome these limitations, hybrid membranes, or so-called mixed matrix membranes, have been proposed as a promising solution – these hybrid systems combine the advantages of both polymeric and inorganic membranes into one system.20 Indeed, various polymers have been modified with inorganic fillers such as zeolites, mesoporous silicas, carbon nanotubes and even non-porous solids to produce hybrid membranes.21-24 Over approximately the last decade, porous crystals such as metal organic frameworks (MOFs) and zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs), have received growing interest as potential fillers in hybrid membranes. MOFs consist of metal cations or clusters linked by organic linkers forming a porous crystalline network.25 ZIFs, a subclass of MOFs, consist of transition metal cations (mainly tetrahedral $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ) bridged by imidazole-based ligands.26 One strong advantage of using MOFs as the inorganic fillers is that MOFs can possess good compatibility with polymer chains due to their partially organic nature, resulting in hybrid membranes with better interfacial wetting.27
In hybrid membrane design, it is crucial to select a filler with appropriate pore size for the relevant separation of interest.28 So far, more than 20,000 types of MOFs have been created with pore sizes ranging from 0.3 to over $10\mathrm{nm}$ .29 However, despite a large selection of MOFs to choose from, there are not often MOFs with ideal pore sizes for many of the most crucial separation challenges today. For example, current MOF technology is not ideal for separating molecules that are very similar in kinetic diameter, like $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (kinetic diameter: $0.33)^{30}$ from ${\bf N}_{2}$ (kinetic diameter: $0.364~\mathrm{nm})^{\mathrm{:}}$ 30; these two molecules differ in kinetic radius by less than $10\%$ . Large-pore MOFs can facilitate rapid diffusion of gas molecules, while smaller pore MOFs may help realize better size-sieving separations but hinder gas transport. For example, the largepore $(>0.5\ \mathrm{nm})$ MOF, UiO- $66\substack{-\Delta\mathrm{V}}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ ${\bf\tilde{\alpha}}{0.6\ n m})$ , has been introduced in hybrid membranes by our group recently to increase $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability, but this was at the sacrifice of some $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity.16 Likewise, hybrid membranes containing small pore ZIFs (ZIF-90, ZIF-8) have been studied elsewhere and display increased $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selectivity over other gases, at the expense of reduced permeability.31-33 Therefore, a new material paradigm is necessary to address this permeability/selectivity trade-off.
Herein, we propose a new architecture for porous crystals to solve separation challenges in hybrid membranes – core/shell MOF systems. Instead of utilizing only one type of MOF as a filler, we report the first successful synthesis and implementation of two types of MOFs into a novel core/shell structural filler for hybrid membrane preparation, in which a large-pore MOF (UiO-66- $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{NH}_{2}\mathbf{\dot{\Psi}}}\end{array}$ ) acts as the core to provide facilitated transport pathways; small-pore ZIF (ZIF-8, effective pore aperture of ${\sim}0.4~\mathrm{nm})^{34}$ or even smaller pores generated at the interface by incommensurate overlapping of the pores of MOF and ZIF, which will act as a molecular filter to enhance molecular sieving properties. An experimental analogy exists in the zeolite filed - it has been proven recently that the reduction of zeolite pore mouths can be achieved by depositing a layer of microporous coating on the external surface.35,36 Similar strategy has also been applied on membrane and catalyst modification to narrow the effective pore size and increase selectivity.37,38 Moreover, the hydrophobic nature of ZIF-8 may provide an additional benefit at the macroscopic level, by more efficiently reducing uptake of water vapor from the flue gas.
The preparation of UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (MOF) was carried out by following a microwave synthetic technique (Supporting Information). Zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , $>98\%$ ) and 2-methylimidazole $(>99\%)$ were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. Methanol $(99.8\%)$ was purchased from BDH Chemicals. The ZIF-8 coating was prepared on the external surface of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ using a layer-by-layer solution deposition method at room temperature by introducing the UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ into the inorganic and organic precursors of ZIF-8 sequentially (and separately) as shown in Figure 1. The amino group on the UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ enables this layer-by-layer processing, by acting as a covalent linker between the MOF and ZIF precursors.39,40 A layer-by-layer method of ZIF-8 preparation was already demonstrated by Eddaoudi on Au substrates, but never before on MOFs.41 Typically, $0.25\mathrm{~g~}$ of UiO-66-NH2 powder was dispersed in
## Nano Letters
inorganic precursors solution of ZIF $(0.219\mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ dissolved in $10\mathrm{mL}$ methanol) with stirring for 2 hours, then washed with methanol. After centrifugation, the inorganic precursor-enriched MOF powder was then immersed in the organic precursor solution of ZIF ( $\mathrm{0.486\g}$ of 2-methylimidazole dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol) with stirring for another 2 hours, and washed with solvent afterwards. The ZIF coated MOF powder was then moved into a $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ oven to evaporate the solvent and form ZIF. This process was carried out once for 1-ZIF $@$ MOF, and cycled 3, 5, and 8 times for 3-ZIF $@$ MOF,5-ZIF $@$ MOF and 8-ZIF $@$ MOF, respectively. Pristine ZIF-8 samples were also synthesized for comparison (Supporting Information).
Before membrane preparation, polysulfone (Udel P-1700, kindly supplied by Solvay Plastics) was degassed at $110~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ under vacuum to remove adsorbed water. For the pure polymer membrane, $0.366\mathrm{g}$ of polysulfone (PSF) was dissolved in $4.0\mathrm{mL}$ of chloroform (BDH Chemicals) and stirred for 24 h to form a viscous solution and subsequently filtered with a $0.2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ PVDF filter (Pall). The fabrication procedure for the hybrid membranes was identical to that of the pure PSF membrane but with an additional dispersion stage. First, the as-synthesized MOF or $Z_{\mathrm{IF}@\mathrm{MOF}}$ was dispersed in chloroform by a tip sonicator (Sonics & Materials). Once dispersed, 1/5 of the PSF solution was added subsequently during sonication to increase interaction and homogeneity between porous fillers and polymer by coating the fillers with a thin polymer layer. The remaining PSF was then added afterwards and sonicated. The solid concentration of final solution was adjusted to around $30\mathrm{wt}\%$ by evaporation of excess solvent to minimize sedimentation. After sonication to remove the air bubbles, the resulting PSF-MOF (hybrid PSF membranes containing pure MOF) or PSF-ZIF $@$ MOF (hybrid PSF membranes containing core/shell ZIF $@$ MOF) solution was cast into a casting plate (Figure 1), loosely covered, and allowed to dry under atmospheric conditions over $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . The dried membranes are then placed into a vacuum oven at $110~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight to remove any residual solvent and water. Hybrid membranes were prepared with fixed loading of $40\mathrm{wt}\%$ but with different ZIF coating cycles. For instance, a defect-free, 7 cm in diameter, hybrid membrane containing $40\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ of 5-cycles ZIF coated MOF (PSF-5-ZIF@MOF, abbreviation will be used in the following description) is shown in Figure 1. The thickness of the final pure and hybrid membranes varied within the range of 60-70 um as measured using a micrometer (Mitutoyo Corp).
Morphologies of the as-prepared UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (MOF) and core/shell ZIF $@$ MOF nanoparticles were examined by transmission electron microscopy (TEM), and scanning electron microscopy (SEM) together with energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS). The crystalline structures of MOF and ZIF $@$ MOF were measured by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) and transmission Wide-Angle X-ray Scattering (WAXS). The functional groups of synthesized MOF, ZIF and ZIF $@$ MOF were further characterized by attenuated total reflection (ATR, PerkinElmer Spectrum One) spectra within the range of $600{-}4000\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The thermal properties of MOF, ZIF and ZIF $@$ MOF were characterized by Q5000 thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) in the range of room temperature to $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with the rate of $10\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . Adsorption experiments of MOF, ZIF, and ZIF $@$ MOF were carried out by Micromeritics ASAP2020 with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and Tristar II (Micromeritics) with ${\bf N}_{2}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ up to a pressure of 1 bar. Before the measurements, the sample was degassed under vacuum at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ . Differential scanning calorimetry (DSC, Q2000, TA Instruments, USA) measurements were performed from 40 to $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere to study the glass transition temperatures $(\mathrm{Tg})$ of the membranes. The water contact angle (CA) was measured at room temperature using Attension Theta Lite (Biolin Scientific) to determine hydrophilic and hydrophobic characteristics of the prepared membranes. Density measurements of the hybrid membranes are conducted with a density determination kit (Mettler Toledo) following Archimedes’ principle (Supporting Information).
Pure gas permeabilities ( $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ ) of PSF, PSF-MOF and PSF-ZIF $@$ MOF membranes were tested using a variable pressure constant volume gas permeation apparatus at $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The membranes are masked with brass discs to accurately define an area where gas transport can occur. Prior to testing, the membranes are degassed within the apparatus overnight. A fixed pressure is applied to the upstream side of the membrane, while the gas flux is recorded as a steady-state pressure rise downstream of the membrane. The gas permeability was calculated according to:
$$
P=\frac{V_{D}l}{p_{2}A R T}(\frac{d p_{1}}{d t})
$$
where $P$ is the gas permeability [1 Barrer $=10^{-10}\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm/(cm^{2}\bullet\ s\bullet\ c m H g)}\$ ], $V_{D}$ represents the downstream volume $(\mathrm{cm}^{3}.$ ), $l$ refers to the film thickness (cm), $p_{2}$ indicates the upstream pressure $\mathrm{(cmHg)}$ ), $A$ is the effective membrane area $(\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ ), $R$ is the gas constant, $T$ is the operating temperature (K), and $d p_{1}/d t$ is steady state pressure rise downstream at fixed upstream pressure $\mathrm{(cmHg/s)}$ ). The ideal selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ can be calculated by the ratio of the permeability of the individual gas.
Figure 2a and 2b showed the high resolution TEM (Supporting Information) images of MOF and core/shell ZIF $@$ MOF. It was observed that, after 5 cycles of ZIF coating, an ultrathin ( ${\sim}20~\mathrm{nm},$ layer of ZIF shell was continuously grown on the MOF surface. High resolution SEM images (Supporting Information) showed that the synthesized MOF and 5-ZIF@MOF are mainly cubic crystals (Figure S1). The smooth surface of the MOF (Figure S1a) becomes much rougher after 5 cycles of ZIF coating (Figure S1b), also suggesting the coverage of ZIF-8 shell on the surface of MOF particles. Both EDS spot scans (labelled as #1) and line scans (labelled as #2) were carried out using Phenom ProX (SEM) to analyze the elemental composition of synthesized MOF (Figure S2) and 5-ZIF $@$ MOF samples (Figure 2c and 2d). It can be seen that the pure MOF is composed of Zr, C and O elements, which is consistent with its structure (Figure S2). The observed Si comes from the substrate. For 5-ZIF $@$ MOF, a new peak of Zn element appears at around $1\mathrm{keV}$ from the spot scan as shown in the inset of Figure 2c, indicating the successful deposition of ZIF coating on the surface of MOF. Figure 2d shows the EDS line scan along the 5-ZIF@MOF particles. The clear Zn signal when compared to the line scan displaced for pure MOF (Figure S2b) further demonstrates the core/shell structure of ZIF $@$ MOF. In order to further prove that the crystalline structure of ZIF-8 shell has be successfully grown on the MOF core, both powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) and transmission Wide-Angle X-ray Scattering (WAXS) on the five samples (UiO-66-NH2 core, 1-ZIF $@$ MOF, 3-ZIF@MOF, 5-ZIF@MOF and 8-ZIF@MOF) have been measured. The results of XRD and WAXS on a Cu K-alpha scale match the crystalline patterns of both UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and ZIF-8 in a range of 2-theta value
## Nano Letters
from 5 to 50 degrees (Figure S3). The resulting peaks corresponding to the (200) plane at 2-theta $\scriptstyle1=10.6$ and (112) plane at 2-theta $\scriptstyle1=12.7$ were detected on the 8-ZIF $@$ MOF sample by XRD and WAXS, proving that the ZIF shell crystal was successfully grown on UiO-66 (Figure S3). Those two representative peaks only emerge in the 8-ZIF $@$ MOF samples, which is due to the fact that the ZIF crystalline peaks require a certain amount of shelling thickness to forming strongly scattering crystalline peaks in this core/shell structure.
ATR analysis of pure UiO-66-NH2, pure ZIF-8 and ZIF $@$ MOF with different ZIF coating cycles were performed as shown in Figure 2e. The spectrum of pure UiO-66- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ matches well with the pattern reported in the literature.42 The peak at around $1620\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponds to the N-H bending vibration.43  The intense doublet at 1415 and $1387\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be assigned to the stretching modes of the carboxylic groups.44 For pure ZIF-8, the peak at $1590~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ is attributed to the $\mathrm{C=N}$ stretching.45 In the lower frequency region, an additional peak at around $1150~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ is observed from ATR spectra for ZIF-8 and ZIF $@$ MOF samples, which is associated with the in-plane bending of imidazole ring in the ZIF-8.45 Moreover, the intensity of this characteristic peak grows stronger with the increase of ZIF cycle numbers, further confirming the successful assembly of ZIF shell around MOF.
Figure 2f shows the experimental $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ of pure UiO-66-NH2, ZIF-8 and ZIF $@$ MOF. UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ showed the highest $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption uptake and ZIF-8 showed the lowest, in good agreement with previous reports.46,47 Therefore, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption uptake of the core/shell ZIF $@$ MOF decreased gradually with more ZIF coating cycles. However, after 5 cycles of ZIF coating, the 5-ZIF $@$ MOF still retained good $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity $\left(2.33\mathrm{\mmol/g}\right.$ at 1 bar, Table S1), only ${\sim}13\%$ lower than the pristine MOF samples, suggesting that the internal large channels of MOF were still maintained for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . But, a significant reduction $(\sim73\%)$ for ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption capacity was observed after 5 cycles of ZIF coating, dropping from $0.157\mathrm{mmol/g}$ (MOF) to $0.043\mathrm{mmol/g}$ (5-ZIF@MOF) at 1 bar. As a result, the ideal $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorptive selectivity (the adsorbed capacity ratio of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ at 1 bar) dramatically increased from 17 to 54 (Table S1). And we speculate that the improved $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorptive selectivity is attributed to the molecular-sieving effect of small pore of ZIF shell or smaller pores generated at the interface between ZIF and MOF surface. The TGA curves of MOF, ZIF-8, and 5-ZIF $@$ MOF are
## Nano Letters
shown in Figure S4. Under $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ TGA showed significant mass loss from solvent evaporation. In the range of 100 to $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , slight weight loss was observed for all the samples due to the desorbed water. With increased temperature, the MOF and $Z_{\mathrm{IF}}@\mathbf{MOF}$ samples will start to decompose at $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , but pure ZIF will not decompose until $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
By using the membrane preparation technique and controlling the layer-by-layer ZIF coating cycle numbers described previously, robust PSF membranes containing $40\mathrm{\mt}\%$ of MOF or ZIF $@$ MOF were successfully fabricated. The morphology of as-prepared membranes was characterized by SEM. Figure 3a and 3d show the cross-sectional SEM images of pure PSF membrane at lower and higher magnifications, respectively. It can be seen that the pure PSF membrane is smooth and continuous. Figure 3b and 3c show the thickness of as-prepared PSF-MOF and PSF-5-ZIF $@$ MOF membranes is highly uniform in the range of $60-70~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ with no sign of pinhole defects. In addition, strong interfacial interaction between fillers and polymer are observed in the membranes (Figure 3e and 3f). To further confirm favorable polymer/porous crystal interactions exist, differential scanning calorimetry measurements were carried out to study the glass transition temperature $(\mathrm{Tg})$ of membranes. Pure PSF membrane shows a $\mathrm{Tg}$ of $186.7^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The incorporation of MOF or ZIF $@$ MOF has triggered a positive shift of Tg by ${\sim}5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S5). This positive shift is due to reduced polymer chain mobility and rigidification as the polymer is adsorbed onto the filler surface, indicating favorable interactions and improved mechanical properties.48 The density of the membrane was calculated and shown in Figure S6. A linear density decrease trend was observed for hybrid membranes as ZIF coating cycles increased due to the smaller density of ZIF-8 $(0.95~\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{cm}^{3})$ ).49 The improvements of surface hydrophobicity of prepared membranes were investigated by contact angle (CA) measurements. As shown in Figure $3\mathrm{g}$ , and 3h, for neat PSF and PSF-MOF membranes, CAs were measured to be $78.5\pm0.5^{\circ}$ and $83.5\pm0.7^{\circ}$ respectively, revealing a hydrophilic property of the surface. However, for PSF-5- Z $\mathrm{IF}@\mathrm{MOF}$ hybrid membrane, the CA increased to around $96^{\circ}$ (Figure 3i), converting the surface from hydrophilic to hydrophobic, due to the good coverage of ZIF-8 shell. Consequently, we expect the PSF$Z{\mathrm{IF}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ hybrid membranes would more efficiently hinder the entrance of water molecules into the membrane in real flue gas capture.
Pure gas permeability and selectivity at $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 3 bar are shown in Figure 4. The neat PSF membrane shows a $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ permeability of 5.6 barrer and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity of 26. After incorporating $40\mathrm{wt}\%$ of MOF $\mathrm{(UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{)}$ ), the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ permeability dramatically increased to 48.2 barrer or $760\%$ higher permeability than the permeability of neat PSF membrane, which is consistent with formation of a percolative network of MOF crystals, as previously reported.16 However, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity of PSF-MOF hybrid membrane decreased to 25, which may result from the poor molecular sieving effect of UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ ( $0.6\mathrm{nm}$ pore size) for distinguishing slightly bigger ${\bf N}_{2}$ molecules over $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . When we applied the external ZIF coating (as shown in Figure 1), after the first 3 cycles (PSF-3-ZIF $@$ MOF), the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity increased to 28 due to the slightly decreased ${\bf N}_{2}$ permeability. When the ZIF coating cycles increased to 5, the ${\bf N}_{2}$ permeability decreased even faster, while maintaining a relatively high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ permeability of 45.2 barrer ( $710\%$ higher than PSF). This resulted in a sharp increase in selectivity to 39 $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2})$ ), which is $50\%$ higher than the neat PSF membrane and $56\%$ higher than PSF-MOF hybrid membranes. As we further increased the ZIF coating to 8 cycles, the high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity was maintained. However, a large decrease of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ permeability was observed. The permeation results suggest that with 5 cycles of ZIF coating, a thin layer of ZIF-8 forms on the external surface of MOF and increases the molecular sieving effect for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over ${\bf N}_{2}$ . After 5 cycles, as we further increased the number of ZIF coating layers, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeation goes down due to the increased transport resistance. Coupled with the large increases in both gas permeability and selectivity, the separation performance of hybrid PSF membranes containing ZIF $@$ MOF as fillers is significantly enhanced over neat PSF membrane and moves much closer towards the Robeson upper bound line (Figure S7). So far, most of the state-of-the-art selective membranes for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ separation exceeding the Robeson upper bound are inorganic membranes, such as NaY, ZSM-5 and SAPO-34 membranes (Figure S7). A comparison of our results to other hybrid PSF membranes in literature is listed in Table 1.16,21-24,50,51 We find that, first, by using the designed dual-channel, molecular-sieving core/shell ZIF $@$ MOF as fillers, the gas permeability and selectivity of as-prepared hybrid PSF membrane can be positively improved simultaneously. Second, without sacrificing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity, our hybrid PSF membrane provides the largest increases in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability. And we expect that the core/shell structural fillers can also be applied, in principle, to other
## Nano Letters
challenging separation problems where no individual MOF structure is effective. Further, we anticipate that these core/shell architectures can be incorporated with other higher performing base polymers to fabricate more competitive hybrid membranes with enhanced separation performance.
In conclusion, we demonstrate for the first time the formation of engineered core/shell structures of MOF, ZIF $@$ MOF, using large pore MOFs as the cores and small pore ZIFs as the shells, then apply the full core/shell particles as porous fillers in hybrid membranes. In this designed ZIF $@$ MOF structure, the large pore MOF acts as a molecular transport highway and the small pore on the external surface works as a molecular filter. Compared with neat PSF polymer membranes, the optimized PSF-ZIF $@$ MOF membranes showed remarkably improved $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ separation performance ( $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability of 45.2 barrer, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity of 39) with $710\%$ higher $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ permeability and $50\%$ higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity. Further, the asprepared membrane exhibited improved surface hydrophobic property. We expect that the incorporation of advanced classes of porous crystals provides a new strategy to make novel hybrid membranes a promising avenue for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture.
